<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cisplatine War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1825">1825</a>–<a href="/years/1828">1828</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cisplatine War was fought from 1825 to 1828 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> and the United Provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> over the Cisplatine Province, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>. The war was costly and unpopular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. It ended with British mediation and the independence of Uruguay, a blow to the prestige of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Emperor Pedro I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cisplatine-war.jpg" alt="Cisplatine War" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See each file for details · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cisplatine-War-collage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The dispute over the Banda Oriental was inherited from the Portuguese and Spanish empires, whose borders in the River Plate basin had never been settled. Portugal founded Colônia do Sacramento in 1680 opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> in 1724 and created the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> in 1776. In 1816 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-kingdom-of-portugal-brazil-and-the-algarves">United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves</a> invaded the Banda Oriental, defeated the resistance led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">José Gervasio Artigas</a> and annexed it as Cisplatina. In 1821 the Montevideo cabildo approved its incorporation into the United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves, and Cisplatina stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> after independence in 1822. A diplomatic mission from the United Provinces in 1823 failed to win a Brazilian withdrawal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1825 a group known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-three-orientals">Thirty-Three Orientals</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-antonio-lavalleja">Juan Antonio Lavalleja</a> and supported by the Argentine government, rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. On 25 August they declared Cisplatina's independence and union with the United Provinces in the Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, and after victories at Rincón and Sarandí the Argentine Congress proclaimed it reintegrated on 25 October. Brazil declared war on 10 December 1825 and blockaded the River Plate.</p><p>The United Provinces occupied the Uruguayan countryside with local insurgents but never took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> or Colonia del Sacramento. The Brazilians could not retake the countryside either, and the largest battle, at Ituzaingó, was indecisive. At sea the Brazilian Navy, with 96 warships, did better, and the small fleet of the Irish-born admiral William Brown was largely destroyed. Both navies relied heavily on British officers and sailors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, the stalemate and public pressure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> led both sides to seek peace, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, whose trade was threatened, pressed them to negotiate. In 1827 the Argentine envoy Manuel José García, exceeding his instructions, signed a treaty renouncing Argentine claims, which President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardino-rivadavia">Bernardino Rivadavia</a> rejected and which contributed to his resignation. Under British mediation, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preliminary-peace-convention">Preliminary Peace Convention</a> signed on 27 August 1828 made Cisplatina an independent state, and the treaty also guaranteed free navigation of the River Plate.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> the cost of the war and the loss of Cisplatina added to the disputes around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Emperor Pedro I</a>, who abdicated in 1831 in favour of his five-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> the national authorities were dissolved, and tensions between Federalists and Unitarians grew. Uruguayans honour the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-three-orientals">Thirty-Three Orientals</a> as national heroes, and Argentines regard William Brown as the father of their navy. In Brazil the war has drawn little interest.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cisplatine War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His ambitions in the Río de la Plata produced the costly <b>Cisplatine War</b> (1825–1828), which ended with the independence of Uruguay.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 25 August 1825 the Thirty-Three Orientals under Juan Antonio Lavalleja declared independence with the backing of the United Provinces of the Río de la Plata, and the 500-day <b>Cisplatine War</b> followed between Brazil and the United Provinces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…1816 that Manuel García had encouraged to end the &quot;federal disease&quot;, passed to Portugal in 1820 and emerged as Uruguay in 1828 after the <b>Cisplatine War</b> and British intervention, by a Treaty of Montevideo that asked no Oriental's opinion and left the borders undrawn, to Brazil's later gain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Returning with troops who had fought in the <b>Cisplatine War</b>, Juan Lavalle of the Unitarian Party mounted a coup in Buenos Aires Province, deposing Governor Manuel Dorrego, whom he captured and killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1825 the Thirty-Three Orientals led by Juan Antonio Lavalleja landed and declared union with the United Provinces of the Río de la Plata, sparking the <b>Cisplatine War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/cisplatina">Cisplatina</a></li></ul></section>
